WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.975 --> 00:00:08.850
VIC IGLESIAS: Challenges
of multI-environment

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:11.110
infrastructure.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:13.200
Let's just go through a
journey for a little bit--

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.950
talk about how we get
to multi-environment

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:15.575
infrastructure.

00:00:15.575 --> 00:00:18.910
Well, first, you start
out with some devs.

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:20.120
They have a laptop.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.250
They have some servers.

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:23.350
So you have two
environments, Dev and Prod.

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:26.260
You push code from your
laptop into your servers.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.800
Everything's fine
for a little bit.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.847
Then you add a bunch
more developers.

00:00:30.847 --> 00:00:31.930
Things get a little hairy.

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:34.940
You start to add some tooling to
make this a little bit easier.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:37.570
So you add some shared
Git repositories

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.250
to control your source code.

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:41.740
You add Jenkins because
you're no longer

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:43.480
SSHing from your laptop.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:47.200
You let Jenkins do the
SSHing because you're smart.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.180
And then, you add an
identity provider, right.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:51.430
You need to manage identity
across all these folks

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:53.990
and have ways to control
what they have access to.

00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:55.990
So we're getting a little
bit more complex here.

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:58.027
But we also added
another environment here

00:00:58.027 --> 00:00:59.860
because we have a lot
of developers that are

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:01.000
trying to integrate their code.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.520
So we added something between
development and production.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:04.970
We added the
staging environment.

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:06.470
So we see these
logical environments

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:08.950
being built all maybe in the
same data center or cloud

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:11.450
provider.

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:14.690
Next, we start adding
even more developers.

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:17.960
We actually hired a security
officer, which is awesome.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:20.880
They start to see a bunch
of our database credentials

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.010
in environment variables.

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:23.990
And they decided to
add some more tooling.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:26.270
They added HashiCorp Vault
to manage our secrets

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:29.210
across our company.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:32.060
Then, eventually, you decide
to move into a cloud provider--

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:33.170
maybe GCP.

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:35.660
How many folks are already
running on GCP here?

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.180
Yes, love you guys.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:41.180
So you add more logical
environments, as well as you're

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:43.370
replicating staging
and prod, potentially,

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:47.170
but you're also adding an entire
different physical location,

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:50.810
right, a cloud providers region
on top of what you already had.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:54.020
And you might start looking
at going from VMs to something

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:54.708
like containers.

00:01:54.708 --> 00:01:56.750
How many folks are doing
containers or Kubernetes

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:58.160
in general?

00:01:58.160 --> 00:01:59.980
Yes, a lot of you.

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:01.883
Welcome.

00:02:01.883 --> 00:02:03.300
So what are some
of the challenges

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.700
that we see as we kind of
go through this journey?

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:08.065
Well, first, we're
deploying different ways

00:02:08.065 --> 00:02:09.690
to different
environments, whether they

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:13.750
be logical or actual physical
different environments.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:15.852
We see that our environments
like staging and prod

00:02:15.852 --> 00:02:17.310
are starting to
drift a little bit.

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:18.790
They don't actually
look the same.

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:20.820
So we're seeing a lot more
things creep into production

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:22.800
that we didn't really
expect because we thought

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.903
staging was going to catch it.

00:02:24.903 --> 00:02:26.570
We have trouble
managing access control.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:29.217
There's different ways to
access different pieces.

00:02:29.217 --> 00:02:31.550
So you start doing broader
and broader permissions sets.

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:33.050
And people can have
access to things

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:34.620
that they shouldn't have.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.440
You also have
networking complexity.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:38.320
Now, you have to
actually go and connect

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.330
to these multiple
physical environments,

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.220
maybe through VPN,
interconnects,

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:43.132
things like that.

00:02:43.132 --> 00:02:43.840
Things get tough.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.830
You have to manage
IP addresses now.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:49.740
Finally, you have
built up a lot of tools

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:51.390
that maybe don't
work well together

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:53.760
or work well with the
infrastructure providers

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.300
that you've put in place.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.217
So you have to start
integrating these things.

00:02:57.217 --> 00:02:58.680
You can't just
throw it all away.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.240
Sometimes you wish you could.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.170
But it's been baked
into your organization.

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:03.795
So you need to start
thinking about how

00:03:03.795 --> 00:03:06.050
to integrate these things.

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:10.220
So this is kind of the view that
we had in the last few slides.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:11.945
We have a lot of
logical environments

00:03:11.945 --> 00:03:14.320
and some physical environments
that we're thinking about.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:17.390
What we want to do is build
a consistent platform out

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:17.990
of these.

00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:21.440
So you ask yourself,
what is the platform?

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:23.540
Essentially, everything
except for what

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:26.570
the developer needs to do to
push code somewhere and get

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:27.260
it running.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.810
You want them to be able
to have the same experience

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:32.272
whether you're in 60 different
environments or three

00:03:32.272 --> 00:03:33.230
different environments.

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:34.190
They push code.

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:36.320
They monitor some sort
of deployment process.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:38.570
And then, eventually, their
code gets out to customers

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:41.462
and starts providing value.

00:03:41.462 --> 00:03:43.420
How many folks have seen
this gentleman before,

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:44.360
Kelsey Hightower?

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:45.460
Yeah.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:46.000
Awesome.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.730
There a tweet from Kelsey
for every single talk

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:49.943
I've ever done.

00:03:49.943 --> 00:03:51.360
And this is the
one for this talk.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.990
Kubernetes is a platform
for building platforms.

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:57.430
Right, it gives us
the starting point

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:00.532
to get an abstraction
across our environments.

00:04:00.532 --> 00:04:01.990
And now, we're
talking about how we

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:04.670
can get there with Kubernetes.

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:06.980
So you'll have different
layers to your stack.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:09.063
You'll have an infrastructure
layer where you have

00:04:09.063 --> 00:04:11.053
disks, compute, networking.

00:04:11.053 --> 00:04:12.470
You'll have a
platform layer where

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:14.803
you'll have these tools that
make it easy for developers

00:04:14.803 --> 00:04:17.420
to get their code out into
those computers and networks

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:18.140
and so forth.

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:19.640
And then you have
your applications.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:21.223
These are the things
that are actually

00:04:21.223 --> 00:04:24.030
giving value to your customers.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:27.100
And so here is kind of
that view of our old,

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:30.020
our previous diagram,
layered into these stacks.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:32.840
So here we have Kubernetes
providing an abstraction

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:34.200
above infrastructure.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.910
In GCP, that's our VPC networks,
our load balancers, our disks,

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:39.050
monitoring, logging--

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:42.380
all these things that
we have to, in the past,

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:43.650
use individually.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:46.410
And you can also use these
across cloud providers,

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:48.190
and even On Prem.

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:51.330
And so then on top of that,
we use that Kubernetes API

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:52.710
to deploy our applications.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:56.640
And you add tools like Jenkins
for CI and Spinnaker for CD.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:59.490
You store your repositories
in Git, maybe GitLab or GitHub

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:00.540
Enterprise.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:02.730
And then you provide
applications to your users.

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:09.060
So what are some of the
benefits of using Kubernetes

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:10.560
at that infrastructure layer?

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.140
Well, first, you get a
consistent deployment platform

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:16.260
that provides you a
single API or set of APIs

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.563
that you can use to manage
your workloads, right.

00:05:18.563 --> 00:05:19.980
When you want to
deploy something,

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:22.950
you know how to deploy that
using an API or declarative

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:24.060
API.

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:27.338
And you get the same type
of deployment process

00:05:27.338 --> 00:05:28.380
when you want to upgrade.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:30.780
So a rolling update,
or a blue green,

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:33.990
you do that one way across
all of your environments.

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:36.118
Finally, this is available
because Kubernetes API

00:05:36.118 --> 00:05:36.660
is available.

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:40.110
It's a managed service across
all of the cloud providers.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.150
So you can instantiate
Kubernetes clusters

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:45.330
in each of them and even
instantiate them On Prem,

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:47.160
with GKE On Prem.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:49.230
So now you have this
one workload API

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:51.150
that you can point your
deployment tools at

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.990
to build your platform.

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:55.930
So now, we've built
this platform.

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:59.410
We have this abstraction
for deploying to Kubernetes.

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:01.120
But one of the
things you'll notice,

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:03.670
as you start onboarding
to Kubernetes,

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:06.520
is you have YAML, and
YAML, and more YAML.

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:10.120
How many folks deal with
YAML on a daily basis?

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:12.460
Lots, lots of us, right.

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:14.252
This gets really
challenging over time.

00:06:14.252 --> 00:06:15.460
There's a lot to think about.

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:17.110
There's a lot of APIs to know.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:19.210
There's pretty good API
documentation out there.

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:22.240
But you need a strategy for
managing these manifests

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:24.320
across the environments.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:26.120
So one way to do this--

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:29.470
sorry, to kind of sum up what
a Kubernetes application is,

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:32.290
or the deployable unit, it's
a lot of these different APIs

00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:33.820
stitched together.

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:37.420
The pods are dependent
on a config map.

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:39.610
And volume the services
depend on pods.

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:41.720
And their labeled so forth.

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:43.780
And they're all defined as YAML.

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:45.400
So what do we really want to do?

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:49.210
We want to have some base
configuration that then we can

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:51.820
add and sprinkle in some
environment specific

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:56.040
configuration on top of-- keep
that minimal as possible--

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:58.440
create a deployable
manifest, and use

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:00.540
that to deploy our
clusters, whether that be

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:06.090
through kube control or
through Spinnaker or whatever.

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:09.547
This is kind of our end goal
with manifest management.

00:07:09.547 --> 00:07:10.880
One way to do this is with Helm.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:13.260
Do we have a lot of
Helm users in here?

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:14.310
Yeah.

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:16.970
So Helm can be seen
as a templating engine

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:18.650
for Kubernetes manifests.

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:20.215
It's got a really
large community.

00:07:20.215 --> 00:07:21.835
And if you're
looking for examples

00:07:21.835 --> 00:07:24.210
of what it looks like to manage
an application with Helm,

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:26.150
you can go to
hub.helm.sh and there

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:28.920
are over 500 applications
that are deployable today

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:30.170
into your Kubernetes clusters.

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:32.798
And you can see what the
patterns are within there.

00:07:32.798 --> 00:07:34.590
It allows you to use
loops and conditionals

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:36.660
within your templates.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:38.910
I'll say that this
can be challenging

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:41.490
as you are now in a touring
complete templating language

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:42.030
mode.

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:44.387
So as people add complexity
to your templates,

00:07:44.387 --> 00:07:46.470
you're going to start to
lose visibility into what

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:48.850
each change actually does.

00:07:48.850 --> 00:07:51.660
And I've experienced this
as a chart maintainer.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:53.790
Sometimes I just have no
idea what a single line

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:56.670
change in a Helm chart
will do to a larger

00:07:56.670 --> 00:08:02.822
kind of multi-file thousands
of line of YAML template.

00:08:02.822 --> 00:08:04.780
The reason I first got
into Helm was because it

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:05.905
was really familiar for me.

00:08:05.905 --> 00:08:08.370
I came from the world
of Chef and Ansible

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:11.253
and knew templating pretty well
and knew how to work with it.

00:08:11.253 --> 00:08:12.420
So that's where I came from.

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:13.620
And it felt really familiar.

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:17.200
So it's a good place to
start for those folks.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:20.780
Helm, if we go to that kind
of base, plus environment

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:25.000
specific config, plus
rendered manifest methodology,

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:27.130
that I mentioned
earlier, Helm allows

00:08:27.130 --> 00:08:28.430
you to define a template.

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:32.780
And so you see those bracketed
values here on the left side.

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:35.470
I have my values dot image
and my values dot log level

00:08:35.470 --> 00:08:37.480
templated into the YAML.

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:39.340
And then I provide
what I want those

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:41.919
values to be rendered into.

00:08:41.919 --> 00:08:43.690
And then it spits
out a template,

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:46.610
a rendered template for me.

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:49.190
I can use the Helm template
command in order to do this,

00:08:49.190 --> 00:08:53.570
and it just spits out the raw
Kubernetes manifest for me.

00:08:53.570 --> 00:08:56.170
Another way to do this is
using a tool called Kustomize

00:08:56.170 --> 00:08:58.650
And it takes a little
bit different approach.

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:02.120
It adds patches to
Kubernetes manifest.

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:06.090
So you bring a normal,
untemplated Kubernetes manifest

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:09.320
and then you apply patches to
that manifest on top of it.

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:11.660
It also can provide
tooling to add labels

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:13.820
across all of your
resources or add

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:15.410
a prefix to all your resources.

00:09:15.410 --> 00:09:18.890
You might add staging to the
front of all of your staging

00:09:18.890 --> 00:09:20.290
manifests, for example.

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:22.470
And it makes it really
easy to do that.

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:24.470
Another benefit of
Kustomize is that it's baked

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:27.242
into Kube Control as of 1.14.

00:09:27.242 --> 00:09:28.700
So if you install
Kube Control 1.14

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:31.420
you already have
Kustomize available.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:33.680
And taking a look at
the model for Kustomize,

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:35.672
you can see it on my
base, I don't really

00:09:35.672 --> 00:09:36.880
have any templating in there.

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:39.700
I could apply this into a
regular Kubernetes cluster

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:41.380
and it would probably work.

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:43.210
And then I add my patches.

00:09:43.210 --> 00:09:45.070
So here, what's
important to know

00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:47.410
is that you have
to tell Kustomize

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:48.950
which thing you're patching.

00:09:48.950 --> 00:09:51.400
So you'll see my patch in
the middle actually calls out

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:54.040
the pod, myapp-pod.

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:56.360
And the container that
I'm going to change.

00:09:56.360 --> 00:09:59.350
And my change here is actually
changing the image from BusyBox

00:09:59.350 --> 00:10:02.860
to busybox to busybox:dev
and does the same thing--

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:05.600
renders me out a template.

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:08.420
So on the right side here,
you see the Kustomization

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:11.670
configuration, and it's
called a Kustomization YAML.

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:14.690
And you can see I add some
common labels, a namespace

00:10:14.690 --> 00:10:17.510
that I want applied to all the
resources, but the core of it

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:20.720
is I say, where's my base or
where are my base manifests?

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:25.090
And then which patches do I
want to apply on top of that?

00:10:25.090 --> 00:10:27.580
Previously, you might have done
a Kube control apply dash F

00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:29.050
in order to use Kustomize.

00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:32.320
You have a Kustomization
YAML in your local directory.

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:36.940
Use Kube control apply dash
K in the local directory

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:42.085
and it will render and
apply the correct manifest.

00:10:42.085 --> 00:10:43.460
The next thing I
wanted to do was

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:46.290
show kind of an example of
reusing as much of your code

00:10:46.290 --> 00:10:47.790
as possible, or as
much of your YAML

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:50.280
as possible, across your
different environments.

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:53.998
So you can imagine a company
that is heavily Java based,

00:10:53.998 --> 00:10:56.540
or has a lot of teams that are
doing a lot of things in Java.

00:10:56.540 --> 00:10:59.560
So they create a base that
is how they would run Java.

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:01.970
This may include setting
some standard XMX

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:05.450
parameters, where logging
goes, some weird dash D flags--

00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:07.520
I don't really do Java.

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:11.210
But you have a lot of stuff that
you bake into this Java base.

00:11:11.210 --> 00:11:13.940
And then from there, you
have different Kustomizations

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:18.020
on top of that Java base that
create an application base.

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:20.060
And then from there,
that application base

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:22.580
can be Kustomized further
for each of the environments

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:23.280
that you're in.

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:25.155
So you're trying to
reuse as much as possible

00:11:25.155 --> 00:11:27.950
as you go through,
so you really know

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:32.180
what the changes are going to
be as you work your way through.

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:36.315
So now that we have a bunch
of YAML that we could deploy,

00:11:36.315 --> 00:11:38.400
we're next going to
bring on Ben Good, who's

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:41.170
going to tell us all about how
to actually deploy this really

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:44.030
good rendered manifest.

00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:46.050
BEN GOOD: OK, so
before we get too far

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:49.050
down into how to deploy, let's
remember where we came from.

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:52.050
So back in day 164 or
whatever, Vic showed us

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:53.550
a picture kind of like this.

00:11:53.550 --> 00:11:56.700
And we were using Spinnaker,
or Jenkins rather,

00:11:56.700 --> 00:12:01.990
to deploy our code
out from our CI system

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:03.240
into our various environments.

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:05.970
And we probably added in
some Chef for some Ansibal,

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:07.950
or something like
that, to help us

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:14.143
keep our environment configs as
stable and similar as possible.

00:12:14.143 --> 00:12:16.560
And then at some point, we
said, oh, this Kubernetes thing

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:17.770
is really cool.

00:12:17.770 --> 00:12:19.380
So let's do some Kubernetes.

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:23.070
And at that point,
your CI pipeline

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:27.480
is pretty similar to what
your application pipeline was.

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:30.810
You're just changing from
building application binaries

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:32.040
to images.

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:36.660
But what's different now is
how you do those deployments.

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:38.610
So now you're
using shell scripts

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:41.610
to run kube control commands
to get your code out

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:44.210
to your various environments.

00:12:44.210 --> 00:12:47.930
At some point, hopefully not
too far from that point, when

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:50.330
you're using shell scripts
to do those deployments,

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:53.630
you realize, I could
really use a tool

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:57.170
that's going to help me
see how my deployments are

00:12:57.170 --> 00:13:01.730
going in a more Kubernetes aware
Kubernetes centric sort of way.

00:13:01.730 --> 00:13:04.700
And that's where
Spinnaker comes in.

00:13:04.700 --> 00:13:08.860
So in Spinnaker, you get
the benefit of Kubernetes

00:13:08.860 --> 00:13:10.610
Awareness, as I like to say.

00:13:10.610 --> 00:13:14.230
So you get visibility
into your replica sets--

00:13:14.230 --> 00:13:18.170
information about how
your pods are performing--

00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:20.500
if they're healthy or not.

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:24.490
In Spinnaker, your
Kubernetes clusters

00:13:24.490 --> 00:13:25.940
are first class citizens.

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:28.090
They're just called
accounts in Spinnaker.

00:13:28.090 --> 00:13:30.920
So Spinnaker knows
about those things.

00:13:30.920 --> 00:13:33.520
And you can use
resource annotations

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:35.920
and then even do
automated rollbacks

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:38.590
to really manage your
deployment pipelines across all

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:41.370
of your environments.

00:13:41.370 --> 00:13:44.590
So samples Spinnaker
pipeline might look

00:13:44.590 --> 00:13:47.450
something kind of like this.

00:13:47.450 --> 00:13:49.480
And it's going to vary
for everyone, depending

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:51.970
on the complexity of
your applications--

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:54.280
how many microservices
you might have.

00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:57.150
But in general, what you're
going to do when you start

00:13:57.150 --> 00:14:02.710
a deployment is to deploy
a canary, so a new version

00:14:02.710 --> 00:14:04.235
of your application.

00:14:04.235 --> 00:14:05.860
You're going to test
it, make sure it's

00:14:05.860 --> 00:14:08.170
working the way you expect
it to from a feature

00:14:08.170 --> 00:14:10.840
functionality
performance standpoint,

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:13.330
validate that thing,
and then roll it out

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:15.940
to production, or to
your staging environment,

00:14:15.940 --> 00:14:17.710
whatever your
deployment target is.

00:14:20.440 --> 00:14:24.130
The canary validation
could be a manual action,

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:27.460
where you say, looks good,
and you click a button.

00:14:27.460 --> 00:14:29.890
At some point, that might
introduce too much latency

00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:30.980
into the process.

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:33.760
So you might want to look
at a tool like Kayenta

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:35.950
to automate the analysis
of your canaries.

00:14:38.780 --> 00:14:40.700
Some important
things to consider

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:42.960
when managing your pipelines.

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:45.290
So treat your pipelines as code.

00:14:45.290 --> 00:14:47.540
I hope that if there's
one thing you take away

00:14:47.540 --> 00:14:49.700
from the deployment
portion of this,

00:14:49.700 --> 00:14:52.820
is that your pipelines
and your CI/CD processes

00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:55.560
are really just code
when it comes down to it.

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:58.790
So if you're applying GitOps
principles to your application

00:14:58.790 --> 00:15:03.200
code, do the same thing for
your application deployment

00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:06.940
pipelines and build processes.

00:15:06.940 --> 00:15:10.180
Try to leverage a tool
like Istio so that way

00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:15.040
you can really understand
where your traffic is heading

00:15:15.040 --> 00:15:17.230
and how much traffic
is hitting your canary

00:15:17.230 --> 00:15:21.280
versus your running deployment.

00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:25.510
And then, use the same
pipeline across all

00:15:25.510 --> 00:15:26.660
of your environments.

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:29.260
So whatever you do for
production deployments,

00:15:29.260 --> 00:15:33.790
should be the same kind of
processes used for staging, QA,

00:15:33.790 --> 00:15:36.280
whatever pre-production
environments you might have.

00:15:38.870 --> 00:15:40.300
Wrong direction.

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:42.800
OK, so in a single
cluster rollout--

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:45.740
we have our shared
tools cluster up there--

00:15:45.740 --> 00:15:48.380
and you start your
rollout by going

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:51.980
to your staging environment,
deploying your canary.

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:55.100
And when it's validated,
you roll it out from there.

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:58.110
Same kind of thing for your
production environment.

00:15:58.110 --> 00:16:01.310
So you deploy your canary, and
then roll it out from there.

00:16:01.310 --> 00:16:05.420
On the other side of the slide
are some deployment strategies

00:16:05.420 --> 00:16:07.020
that you could use.

00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:10.850
But typically you find yourself
in the rollout scenario

00:16:10.850 --> 00:16:13.310
because it's more
resource friendly.

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:20.400
OK, now, let's think
about this from

00:16:20.400 --> 00:16:22.440
a multi-cluster perspective.

00:16:22.440 --> 00:16:27.480
So because Kubernetes is
a cross platform tool,

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:32.400
you can be running GKE clusters,
you can be running GKE On Prem,

00:16:32.400 --> 00:16:36.430
and you could be running
Kubernetes in some other cloud.

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:41.790
And that introduces several
complications to this scenario

00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:43.200
here.

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:46.020
Notice in the slide, we
have two GKE clusters

00:16:46.020 --> 00:16:49.570
that look pretty similar but
they're in different locations.

00:16:49.570 --> 00:16:53.100
We have a GKE On Prem with a
different version of Kubernetes

00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:56.910
and another cloud provider out
there with yet another version.

00:16:56.910 --> 00:17:00.630
And you have to think about
how your application behaves

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:02.380
in those scenarios.

00:17:02.380 --> 00:17:05.339
And then also about
how your interconnects

00:17:05.339 --> 00:17:07.710
are happening there because
they can introduce variables

00:17:07.710 --> 00:17:11.450
into the scenarios.

00:17:11.450 --> 00:17:15.940
So again, with Spinnaker,
you can chain your rollouts,

00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:20.050
your deployments, so you start
with a single cluster, deploy

00:17:20.050 --> 00:17:24.339
your canary to that one cluster,
and that one looks good.

00:17:24.339 --> 00:17:27.910
Then roll it out to the rest
of the fleet in that cluster.

00:17:27.910 --> 00:17:30.480
And then move on
to the next one.

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:32.520
Maybe your monitoring
and your tooling

00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:36.030
is sufficient that you can
do your canary roll out

00:17:36.030 --> 00:17:38.580
to every cluster all
at the same time.

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:42.390
But understand that you're
looking at the results

00:17:42.390 --> 00:17:45.510
from your canary
deployment in the context

00:17:45.510 --> 00:17:48.390
of many other variables
at the same time.

00:17:48.390 --> 00:17:52.020
So but the important part
is to canary on each one

00:17:52.020 --> 00:17:54.210
because those
differences could have

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:55.950
an impact on your application.

00:17:55.950 --> 00:18:01.240
And also to use the same
process for each cluster.

00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:04.150
So once when you have your
application out there,

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:07.020
you also need to be thinking
about how to manage traffic

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:08.760
across those clusters.

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:12.840
So Istio is a good tool to
use for managing your intra

00:18:12.840 --> 00:18:17.250
cluster with M1 cluster
or across your clusters,

00:18:17.250 --> 00:18:20.310
or also known as your
east west traffic.

00:18:20.310 --> 00:18:22.230
The other thing that's
really helpful to do

00:18:22.230 --> 00:18:24.940
is to manage your
north south traffic.

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:28.050
So how much traffic you're
getting from the outside

00:18:28.050 --> 00:18:31.170
to a particular
Kubernetes cluster.

00:18:31.170 --> 00:18:34.380
And tools like
Cloudflare, or Akamai,

00:18:34.380 --> 00:18:37.050
or Fastly, something
like that, can

00:18:37.050 --> 00:18:39.420
help you manage that traffic.

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:44.280
And that allows you to cluster
to canary your application

00:18:44.280 --> 00:18:45.490
at the cluster level.

00:18:45.490 --> 00:18:49.200
So you could do this upgrade
that's shown on the slide

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:52.860
and know that that
new change isn't going

00:18:52.860 --> 00:18:55.220
to impact your application.

00:18:55.220 --> 00:18:57.400
So once when all those
things are in place,

00:18:57.400 --> 00:19:00.730
you're in a pretty good
position to handle changes

00:19:00.730 --> 00:19:06.220
to your environments and to
your Kubernetes clusters.

00:19:06.220 --> 00:19:09.860
For example, GKE has this
awesome little upgrade button.

00:19:09.860 --> 00:19:14.020
So when you upgrade, it goes out
and it rolls out the changes--

00:19:14.020 --> 00:19:17.260
the new versions of Kubernetes
to all of your masters

00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:19.030
and worker notes.

00:19:19.030 --> 00:19:20.180
And that's awesome.

00:19:20.180 --> 00:19:21.740
It's a great feature.

00:19:21.740 --> 00:19:24.850
But that also introduced
changes in the API.

00:19:24.850 --> 00:19:26.320
And if that does
cause a problem,

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:32.500
rolling back is hard in that
scenario, or it could be hard.

00:19:32.500 --> 00:19:35.710
But having the ability to
control those, your traffic

00:19:35.710 --> 00:19:40.900
flows, and what versions of your
applications are deployed where

00:19:40.900 --> 00:19:43.430
is very useful.

00:19:43.430 --> 00:19:46.000
Also like I
mentioned previously,

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:50.320
Kubernetes clusters are built
in and first class citizens

00:19:50.320 --> 00:19:51.640
in Spinnaker.

00:19:51.640 --> 00:19:55.360
So they plug right
in relatively nicely.

00:19:55.360 --> 00:19:56.980
and Karl is going
to touch on this

00:19:56.980 --> 00:20:00.490
in a little bit about some
of the nuances and challenges

00:20:00.490 --> 00:20:01.720
there.

00:20:01.720 --> 00:20:04.390
But they fit in pretty nicely.

00:20:04.390 --> 00:20:09.100
And then also, don't forget
that shared cluster up there

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:12.490
with all of your Jenkins, and
your Spinnaker, your Vault,

00:20:12.490 --> 00:20:15.710
whatever those shared
tools across your system.

00:20:15.710 --> 00:20:18.220
Be sure to treat all
that stuff like codes,

00:20:18.220 --> 00:20:21.070
keep it in source
control, and also try

00:20:21.070 --> 00:20:24.790
to store your artifacts
from those processes

00:20:24.790 --> 00:20:27.370
out somewhere else,
not on the cluster.

00:20:27.370 --> 00:20:30.560
That keeps you out of
the pet cluster scenario

00:20:30.560 --> 00:20:34.720
that we hope no one
finds themselves in.

00:20:34.720 --> 00:20:37.150
And the Google
Cloud Storage plugin

00:20:37.150 --> 00:20:40.520
is a pretty good way
to go about doing that.

00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:43.220
So now, I'm going to
turn it over to Karl.

00:20:43.220 --> 00:20:47.210
KARL ISENBERG: Thank you, I
am Karl, and I work at Cruise.

00:20:47.210 --> 00:20:51.320
And Cruise is building
self-driving vehicles

00:20:51.320 --> 00:20:53.658
to safely connect people
to the places, and things,

00:20:53.658 --> 00:20:55.200
and experiences that
they care about.

00:20:55.850 --> 00:20:58.940
So we're primarily building
a self-driving car fleet

00:20:58.940 --> 00:21:00.560
in San Francisco right now.

00:21:00.560 --> 00:21:03.866
And this is one of our open
source tools called World View.

00:21:03.866 --> 00:21:06.860
It allows us to see
what the car sees

00:21:06.860 --> 00:21:10.290
and be able to reproduce
its functionality.

00:21:10.290 --> 00:21:12.710
So I work on the Paths team.

00:21:12.710 --> 00:21:14.390
So I don't work
on the car itself,

00:21:14.390 --> 00:21:17.222
mostly just on the
cloud backend stuff.

00:21:17.222 --> 00:21:18.680
A lot of the stuff
that I deal with

00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:20.930
has to do with the
multi-cloud infrastructure

00:21:20.930 --> 00:21:22.340
that we have at Cruise.

00:21:22.340 --> 00:21:28.250
We have hybrid, private cloud
internet, or network rather,

00:21:28.250 --> 00:21:31.280
that spans on
premise GCP and AWS.

00:21:31.280 --> 00:21:33.440
We have things On
Premise that need

00:21:33.440 --> 00:21:36.080
to be On Premise like
ingestion from the car data.

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:40.850
We have stuff in GCP that
makes sense on GCP, like GPU,

00:21:40.850 --> 00:21:42.800
and machine learning, and GKE.

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:46.100
We have stuff on AWS that
we've had on AWS for a while

00:21:46.100 --> 00:21:49.740
and that makes sense on AWS
based on their strengths.

00:21:49.740 --> 00:21:52.070
So this is just a
sampling of our sort

00:21:52.070 --> 00:21:55.168
of multi-cloud infrastructure
and the variety of workloads

00:21:55.168 --> 00:21:55.960
that we're running.

00:21:55.960 --> 00:22:00.350
It's kind of wider than most
places that I've worked at.

00:22:00.350 --> 00:22:03.140
So in order to make
this work on GCP,

00:22:03.140 --> 00:22:05.270
which I'm going to
be focusing on today,

00:22:05.270 --> 00:22:11.000
we ended up splitting up
our projects and our folders

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:13.520
in order to separate them
on environment lines.

00:22:13.520 --> 00:22:16.970
We have environmental
folders at the top level

00:22:16.970 --> 00:22:18.800
of the organization in GCP.

00:22:18.800 --> 00:22:21.800
And then we also separate the
networks along those lines

00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:25.580
as well to provide isolation
between development,

00:22:25.580 --> 00:22:28.040
and staging, and production.

00:22:28.040 --> 00:22:32.270
So if someone compromised
development on some CI

00:22:32.270 --> 00:22:34.220
from open source
tooling or something,

00:22:34.220 --> 00:22:36.680
they don't have access to
the production clusters

00:22:36.680 --> 00:22:38.000
or their workloads there.

00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:41.030
So it adds a little extra
security, a little reduction

00:22:41.030 --> 00:22:42.350
of blast radius.

00:22:42.350 --> 00:22:43.940
And what this
means is that we're

00:22:43.940 --> 00:22:47.640
setting up multiple projects
per team at this point.

00:22:47.640 --> 00:22:52.250
So for example, NetEng here
has a shared private network

00:22:52.250 --> 00:22:54.000
spanning multiple environments.

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:56.180
And they share that network
with the other projects.

00:22:56.180 --> 00:22:58.780
And we have different
teams that have,

00:22:58.780 --> 00:23:01.760
for example, their
databases in SAS products

00:23:01.760 --> 00:23:06.320
and other things like Cloud
SQL, other SAS products in GCE,

00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:09.170
whether they're running
GCE nodes or GCP SAS.

00:23:09.170 --> 00:23:11.870
And then the PaaS
team manages a set

00:23:11.870 --> 00:23:14.570
of shared clusters across
the environment here.

00:23:17.250 --> 00:23:20.270
So in order to make this
sort of multi-tenancy,

00:23:20.270 --> 00:23:24.710
multi-environment workload
or spread work in GKE,

00:23:24.710 --> 00:23:27.530
we also spread
these environments

00:23:27.530 --> 00:23:29.490
across the GKE namespaces.

00:23:29.490 --> 00:23:33.290
So we use GKE namespaces
for a tendency

00:23:33.290 --> 00:23:38.150
that is not necessarily full
multi-tenancy, but also not

00:23:38.150 --> 00:23:40.520
completely unisolated.

00:23:40.520 --> 00:23:42.440
So we span these-- we
have these clusters

00:23:42.440 --> 00:23:44.732
in these different environments,
and we have namespaces

00:23:44.732 --> 00:23:47.450
allocated to different
teams across those clusters.

00:23:47.450 --> 00:23:50.270
So a different team would
use continuous deployment,

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:53.210
for example, to migrate their
application from development

00:23:53.210 --> 00:23:57.620
to staging to production, as
it increases in instability.

00:23:57.620 --> 00:24:00.950
So a couple of security
challenges this presented to us

00:24:00.950 --> 00:24:03.530
is that when you're not
using one Kubernetes

00:24:03.530 --> 00:24:05.180
environment or one
GKE environment,

00:24:05.180 --> 00:24:08.893
you have to figure out how to
make sure that your enterprise,

00:24:08.893 --> 00:24:11.060
and your identity provider,
and your single sign on,

00:24:11.060 --> 00:24:12.740
and your service accounts,
and your credential

00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:14.115
rotation, and your
bootstrapping,

00:24:14.115 --> 00:24:17.355
and your project works
across those clusters.

00:24:17.355 --> 00:24:18.980
And effectively, what
that meant for us

00:24:18.980 --> 00:24:21.260
is that we had to
go from managing

00:24:21.260 --> 00:24:24.050
a whole bunch of clusters
to managing a smaller

00:24:24.050 --> 00:24:27.910
set of clusters with having
a platform team that manages

00:24:27.910 --> 00:24:30.500
those, so that we could
centralize that experience so

00:24:30.500 --> 00:24:33.800
that we could level up the
experience that everybody had.

00:24:33.800 --> 00:24:36.440
So there's some other security
concerns around encryption

00:24:36.440 --> 00:24:40.250
that I'll get into in a
bit, but basically, you

00:24:40.250 --> 00:24:42.680
need secret management,
and then secret injection,

00:24:42.680 --> 00:24:45.920
and some kind of caching
for secret sometimes,

00:24:45.920 --> 00:24:48.153
and you need to manage
TLS across your clusters.

00:24:48.153 --> 00:24:49.820
If you have it in
more than one cluster,

00:24:49.820 --> 00:24:51.570
it becomes a little
bit more of a headache

00:24:51.570 --> 00:24:53.410
to manage externally.

00:24:53.410 --> 00:24:55.160
So this is one of the
things you might not

00:24:55.160 --> 00:24:57.493
think about when you start
going into multi-environment,

00:24:57.493 --> 00:25:01.040
is that multi-environment really
probably means multiple GKE

00:25:01.040 --> 00:25:01.542
clusters.

00:25:01.542 --> 00:25:03.500
And when you're thinking
multiple GKE clusters,

00:25:03.500 --> 00:25:05.780
you can't centralize
everything in the CRD level.

00:25:05.780 --> 00:25:08.450
You can't centralize everything
in the Kubernetes layer.

00:25:08.450 --> 00:25:10.710
It doesn't make sense to
store the source of truth

00:25:10.710 --> 00:25:12.230
in one GKE secrets.

00:25:12.230 --> 00:25:14.900
You probably want to externalize
those, and then replicate

00:25:14.900 --> 00:25:17.870
those to your GKE clusters.

00:25:17.870 --> 00:25:21.020
And then we have a whole bunch
of complications around access

00:25:21.020 --> 00:25:24.330
control, around group
management, and role binding,

00:25:24.330 --> 00:25:26.070
which I'll get into in a bit.

00:25:26.070 --> 00:25:30.000
And then also like service
to service authentication,

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:32.000
access control is a
little challenging.

00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:34.750
User to service, user to
platform, user through service.

00:25:34.750 --> 00:25:36.500
All of these are things
that you might not

00:25:36.500 --> 00:25:37.958
think of right up
front, but you'll

00:25:37.958 --> 00:25:40.707
have to solve sooner or later.

00:25:40.707 --> 00:25:42.290
So one of the things
I wanted to focus

00:25:42.290 --> 00:25:44.720
on that sort of challenges
from the security perspective

00:25:44.720 --> 00:25:46.580
is Vault infrastructure.

00:25:46.580 --> 00:25:49.430
We chose Vault early on,
partly because we were already

00:25:49.430 --> 00:25:51.680
using it, and partly because
we wanted to externalize,

00:25:51.680 --> 00:25:56.120
like I was saying, the secret's
management outside of GKE.

00:25:56.120 --> 00:26:01.340
We do like the GKE secrets,
but they're a little coupled

00:26:01.340 --> 00:26:04.440
with names to base
permission boundaries,

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:07.320
and we wanted to bind
them externally to that.

00:26:07.320 --> 00:26:10.400
So a couple of the solutions
that we targeted here

00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:14.280
was a Vault log-in through
Kubernetes service account.

00:26:14.280 --> 00:26:16.490
So you can use your service
account in Kubernetes

00:26:16.490 --> 00:26:18.170
to log into Vault,
and that means

00:26:18.170 --> 00:26:20.210
you have to authorize
that service

00:26:20.210 --> 00:26:22.010
account with
permissions involved,

00:26:22.010 --> 00:26:23.370
and that's managed externally.

00:26:23.370 --> 00:26:27.890
And then also if you want to
go all the way into secrets

00:26:27.890 --> 00:26:30.500
management here, you have to
get into either secret injection

00:26:30.500 --> 00:26:31.790
or secret caching.

00:26:31.790 --> 00:26:34.100
We went with secrets
injection, and that

00:26:34.100 --> 00:26:36.200
meant using this model
on the left, which

00:26:36.200 --> 00:26:40.100
is an init container that we
wrote, or you can write pretty

00:26:40.100 --> 00:26:43.700
trivially, that basically
reads from Vault

00:26:43.700 --> 00:26:45.500
logs in with the service
account, downloads

00:26:45.500 --> 00:26:47.833
the secrets that are configured
in environment variable,

00:26:47.833 --> 00:26:51.260
stores them in memory
volume so that they're not

00:26:51.260 --> 00:26:54.357
written to disk, and then the
app container slurps them in.

00:26:54.357 --> 00:26:56.690
So this is a little different
than environment variables

00:26:56.690 --> 00:26:58.357
because you have to
read them from disk,

00:26:58.357 --> 00:27:00.080
but it's not too
hard to read those

00:27:00.080 --> 00:27:03.350
in from a bootstrap
or an entry point

00:27:03.350 --> 00:27:05.430
in your Docker file
in your container.

00:27:05.430 --> 00:27:10.970
So basically, this lets
us do lift and shift,

00:27:10.970 --> 00:27:13.430
where we have a container
that doesn't know about Vault,

00:27:13.430 --> 00:27:15.830
and then inject the
secrets into it.

00:27:15.830 --> 00:27:18.950
But an alternative way is to
make your container of Vault

00:27:18.950 --> 00:27:21.170
aware, and pull from
Vault at runtime.

00:27:21.170 --> 00:27:22.820
That's also an
option, which we have

00:27:22.820 --> 00:27:24.930
used in a couple of scenarios.

00:27:24.930 --> 00:27:27.500
So GCP service accounts is
another integration in Vault

00:27:27.500 --> 00:27:30.020
that we've enabled
and configured,

00:27:30.020 --> 00:27:34.280
which allows us to log in from
the container with my Vault

00:27:34.280 --> 00:27:38.600
credentials to ask Vault
for a GCP service account,

00:27:38.600 --> 00:27:41.120
and then it asks it for
specific credentials that

00:27:41.120 --> 00:27:43.500
are generated with a timeout.

00:27:43.500 --> 00:27:46.590
So your application gets
credentials to talk to GCP to,

00:27:46.590 --> 00:27:49.400
for example, talk to your
database in Cloud SQL,

00:27:49.400 --> 00:27:56.130
or talk to your SQL in big
data, or something like that.

00:27:56.130 --> 00:27:59.497
So we use the sort of
host of this solution,

00:27:59.497 --> 00:28:00.830
and there's a couple other ones.

00:28:00.830 --> 00:28:04.970
There's an AWS integration,
there's several other pieces,

00:28:04.970 --> 00:28:06.150
and Vault is very powerful.

00:28:06.150 --> 00:28:08.510
But what this ends up
meaning is that you

00:28:08.510 --> 00:28:12.060
need to do a little bit of
Vault policy management.

00:28:12.060 --> 00:28:16.070
So we ended up with a sort of
convention around Vault name

00:28:16.070 --> 00:28:18.950
spacing that matches
our GKE namespaces,

00:28:18.950 --> 00:28:21.590
which matches our
GCP projects, and it

00:28:21.590 --> 00:28:23.930
means that your
applications are stored--

00:28:23.930 --> 00:28:26.090
or the secrets that the
application use are stored

00:28:26.090 --> 00:28:28.730
in Vault in a place
that is namespace aware,

00:28:28.730 --> 00:28:31.320
that's environment aware,
and its application aware.

00:28:31.320 --> 00:28:34.170
So what this allows you
to do is, for example,

00:28:34.170 --> 00:28:37.580
have a team admin or
team members that go

00:28:37.580 --> 00:28:40.220
and have permission to read,
and write, and delete secrets

00:28:40.220 --> 00:28:43.888
on all the
environments, and then

00:28:43.888 --> 00:28:46.430
you can have other people with
read permissions to those that

00:28:46.430 --> 00:28:49.220
don't necessarily have
access to see them,

00:28:49.220 --> 00:28:51.900
but you can just list them
to show that they exist.

00:28:51.900 --> 00:28:53.600
And then you can
have the application

00:28:53.600 --> 00:28:55.768
that only knows about
its environments

00:28:55.768 --> 00:28:58.310
specific credentials, that way
you're not leaking credentials

00:28:58.310 --> 00:28:59.970
across environments.

00:28:59.970 --> 00:29:02.570
One of the nice things
that this provides us with

00:29:02.570 --> 00:29:04.610
is it means that our
continuous deployment

00:29:04.610 --> 00:29:08.040
solution doesn't need to know
about the application secrets.

00:29:08.040 --> 00:29:10.370
It means the application
developer can update

00:29:10.370 --> 00:29:12.830
the application secrets
independent of the life

00:29:12.830 --> 00:29:15.347
cycle of the
application, and it means

00:29:15.347 --> 00:29:17.180
that your continuous
deployment doesn't need

00:29:17.180 --> 00:29:20.930
to be authorized with Vault.

00:29:20.930 --> 00:29:23.390
So a couple of challenges
that we went to.

00:29:23.390 --> 00:29:25.850
I'll just sort categorize
and bucket these.

00:29:25.850 --> 00:29:29.090
Image building and
deployment is sort

00:29:29.090 --> 00:29:30.983
of a problem we need
to solve everywhere,

00:29:30.983 --> 00:29:33.150
and if you solve this in a
multi-tenant environment,

00:29:33.150 --> 00:29:34.640
it's a little more complicated.

00:29:34.640 --> 00:29:38.300
If you solve it in a
multi-environment scenario,

00:29:38.300 --> 00:29:40.640
it's not necessarily
much different.

00:29:40.640 --> 00:29:43.190
The way we went
with is having a GCR

00:29:43.190 --> 00:29:47.360
that's central to the
PaaS team that hosts GCR.

00:29:47.360 --> 00:29:49.490
But what that means is
that because the boundaries

00:29:49.490 --> 00:29:52.160
for security are
around GCR, that

00:29:52.160 --> 00:29:57.560
means everybody who has
access to push or delete to

00:29:57.560 --> 00:30:00.200
that GCR also has permission
to delete other people's

00:30:00.200 --> 00:30:02.672
containers that they've
pushed into that GCR, which

00:30:02.672 --> 00:30:04.130
is a little bit of
a security risk.

00:30:04.130 --> 00:30:06.740
So you can either
centralize this,

00:30:06.740 --> 00:30:09.650
or you can spread it out,
delegate it to the teams

00:30:09.650 --> 00:30:11.400
so that each team is
hosting their own.

00:30:11.400 --> 00:30:14.270
But if you do that, then you
have to install a pole secret

00:30:14.270 --> 00:30:15.980
on all of your containers--

00:30:15.980 --> 00:30:17.790
sorry, on all of your clusters.

00:30:17.790 --> 00:30:20.210
And if you're running
multiple clusters,

00:30:20.210 --> 00:30:23.330
that can be a
little complicated.

00:30:23.330 --> 00:30:26.090
So around application
operations,

00:30:26.090 --> 00:30:29.060
some of the things that GKE
doesn't really provide you

00:30:29.060 --> 00:30:31.160
with here have to be
solved, whether you're

00:30:31.160 --> 00:30:32.600
in one environment or not.

00:30:32.600 --> 00:30:37.850
That includes DNS configuration,
TLS configuration,

00:30:37.850 --> 00:30:40.040
structured logging
will automatically

00:30:40.040 --> 00:30:42.050
get converted by
stack drivers, but you

00:30:42.050 --> 00:30:45.590
have to actually omit structured
logging from your application.

00:30:45.590 --> 00:30:48.470
Using the right
metrics library is also

00:30:48.470 --> 00:30:51.660
important for Stackdriver,
and in our case,

00:30:51.660 --> 00:30:53.698
we use Datadog to omit
our logs to Datadog

00:30:53.698 --> 00:30:55.490
because that's where
we were conventionally

00:30:55.490 --> 00:30:57.510
aggregating all of our data.

00:30:57.510 --> 00:30:59.248
And then around
lifecycle, this is

00:30:59.248 --> 00:31:01.040
where we get back to
continuous integration

00:31:01.040 --> 00:31:02.600
and continuous deployment.

00:31:02.600 --> 00:31:06.170
The sort of templating and
cloud development environment,

00:31:06.170 --> 00:31:09.350
we allow people to
develop locally,

00:31:09.350 --> 00:31:12.237
but we find, in general, people
prefer developing in the cloud.

00:31:12.237 --> 00:31:13.820
So they have more
resources available.

00:31:13.820 --> 00:31:15.303
It's more like production.

00:31:15.303 --> 00:31:16.970
They don't have to
have a beefy machine.

00:31:16.970 --> 00:31:18.500
They can just do
it on their laptop.

00:31:18.500 --> 00:31:21.030
So we provide a cloud
host and dev environment.

00:31:21.030 --> 00:31:23.120
We host a staging
environment, which

00:31:23.120 --> 00:31:27.680
we allow use for both near prod
deployment and load testing.

00:31:27.680 --> 00:31:29.150
And then integration
can either be

00:31:29.150 --> 00:31:31.040
spread across dev or
staging, depending

00:31:31.040 --> 00:31:34.220
on where your CI is located.

00:31:34.220 --> 00:31:37.310
With the use of Spinnaker,
you can get approval gates.

00:31:37.310 --> 00:31:41.650
Blue/green isn't quite supported
yet, but deploy targets is.

00:31:41.650 --> 00:31:45.310
And then account management
I'll get to it in a minute.

00:31:45.310 --> 00:31:48.220
So one of the solutions you
can get to for image building

00:31:48.220 --> 00:31:49.990
is to use Knative build.

00:31:49.990 --> 00:31:53.270
We're not quite using this
yet, but we prototyped it.

00:31:53.270 --> 00:31:55.120
And one of the things
we liked about it

00:31:55.120 --> 00:31:57.400
is that unlike the
rest of Knative.

00:31:57.400 --> 00:32:01.473
You don't need Istio to use
the build portion of Knative.

00:32:01.473 --> 00:32:02.890
And so what that
means is that you

00:32:02.890 --> 00:32:08.830
can get a CRD to push builds as
CRD Yammer files, and you can

00:32:08.830 --> 00:32:12.520
do that from CI, and
it'll push the build

00:32:12.520 --> 00:32:15.922
from permissions that Knative
build has into your GCR,

00:32:15.922 --> 00:32:18.130
and that means we don't
actually have to give out GCR

00:32:18.130 --> 00:32:20.450
push to any of our developers.

00:32:20.450 --> 00:32:21.910
So what we've done
is we have a dev

00:32:21.910 --> 00:32:23.420
GCR that anyone
can push through,

00:32:23.420 --> 00:32:24.670
and then we have a
prod GCR that you

00:32:24.670 --> 00:32:26.770
have to have a service
account to push to, or push

00:32:26.770 --> 00:32:30.560
to from a build environment.

00:32:30.560 --> 00:32:33.003
Another thing we did was
we've been using Spinnaker

00:32:33.003 --> 00:32:34.420
for a while, and
one of the things

00:32:34.420 --> 00:32:38.060
we realized that it was a
real pain to add new accounts.

00:32:38.060 --> 00:32:40.390
So when we got to this
multi-environment, multi-tenant

00:32:40.390 --> 00:32:45.300
system, and we had somewhere
between three and 20 clusters,

00:32:45.300 --> 00:32:47.950
and then we decided, you
know, I don't want everyone

00:32:47.950 --> 00:32:50.920
to have cluster admin to
deploy into whatever namespace

00:32:50.920 --> 00:32:52.510
they want from Spinnaker.

00:32:52.510 --> 00:32:54.080
We actually had to
isolate that down.

00:32:54.080 --> 00:32:57.010
So now we're spitting
out Spinnaker accounts

00:32:57.010 --> 00:32:59.433
by like 20 per
cluster, which meant

00:32:59.433 --> 00:33:00.850
we needed a new
way to manage this

00:33:00.850 --> 00:33:04.105
because each new account
requires a reboot of Spinnaker,

00:33:04.105 --> 00:33:08.560
a redeploy because the admin
configuration is in JSON,

00:33:08.560 --> 00:33:11.330
actually, under Halyard,
which manages Spinnaker.

00:33:11.330 --> 00:33:14.300
So what we did was we
wrote a Spinnaker operator,

00:33:14.300 --> 00:33:16.360
and the Spinnaker operator
basically just manages

00:33:16.360 --> 00:33:19.400
your accounts, and manages the
lifecycle of Spinnaker itself.

00:33:19.400 --> 00:33:22.300
So this is not yet
open source, but we're

00:33:22.300 --> 00:33:23.800
leaning in that
direction, and we're

00:33:23.800 --> 00:33:25.842
going to use it in production
for a little longer

00:33:25.842 --> 00:33:27.490
to make sure we've
vetted it right,

00:33:27.490 --> 00:33:29.740
and then we're going to
probably put it in open source

00:33:29.740 --> 00:33:32.600
in the following months.

00:33:32.600 --> 00:33:35.240
Some other networking
challenges we ran into--

00:33:35.240 --> 00:33:36.590
DNS, I mentioned.

00:33:36.590 --> 00:33:40.980
Public DNS turns out
to be pretty easy,

00:33:40.980 --> 00:33:43.430
but when you start
doing private networking

00:33:43.430 --> 00:33:45.160
or hybrid private
networking, you

00:33:45.160 --> 00:33:48.740
get the word about private
zones, which is a brand new GCP

00:33:48.740 --> 00:33:49.970
feature.

00:33:49.970 --> 00:33:53.050
And then private DNS
from the cluster,

00:33:53.050 --> 00:33:54.800
you have to start
worrying about if you're

00:33:54.800 --> 00:33:57.380
going across
interconnects or VPNs,

00:33:57.380 --> 00:33:59.300
you have to worry about
configuring the IP

00:33:59.300 --> 00:34:02.900
mask on your cube
DNS or your core DNS,

00:34:02.900 --> 00:34:05.220
depending on your config.

00:34:05.220 --> 00:34:09.110
And this allows you to propagate
the IPs from multiple networks,

00:34:09.110 --> 00:34:11.844
otherwise all you know
about is the GCP networks.

00:34:11.844 --> 00:34:13.219
For Ingress, I'm
going to go over

00:34:13.219 --> 00:34:15.719
a pretty complicated private
Ingress solution in the future,

00:34:15.719 --> 00:34:16.909
or in the next slide.

00:34:16.909 --> 00:34:20.540
And public Ingress, we mostly
just use GCLB, however,

00:34:20.540 --> 00:34:25.280
you have to supply
your own TLS and DNS.

00:34:25.280 --> 00:34:28.460
For Egress, public Egress--

00:34:28.460 --> 00:34:30.980
private egress,
sorry, goes through--

00:34:30.980 --> 00:34:32.480
if you have a
private network, it

00:34:32.480 --> 00:34:34.370
goes through your VPN
or your interconnects.

00:34:34.370 --> 00:34:35.870
You have to set up
your Cloud Router

00:34:35.870 --> 00:34:39.710
to basically configure
IP propagation

00:34:39.710 --> 00:34:41.542
across the clouds
in both directions,

00:34:41.542 --> 00:34:43.250
which is a little
complicated, especially

00:34:43.250 --> 00:34:46.260
if you start getting into
multiple networks per GCP,

00:34:46.260 --> 00:34:51.770
and multiple clouds on prem, and
I'm glad we have a NetEng team.

00:34:51.770 --> 00:34:56.030
So public Egress,
though we previously

00:34:56.030 --> 00:34:57.620
deployed our own
nets with Terraform

00:34:57.620 --> 00:34:59.662
because that was the
recommended solution on GCP,

00:34:59.662 --> 00:35:03.790
there is now a Cloud NAT
solution that is beta--

00:35:03.790 --> 00:35:06.050
I think it's GA.

00:35:06.050 --> 00:35:08.900
And so we are switching
to that, but what it means

00:35:08.900 --> 00:35:12.080
is you have to put network
tags per your regions

00:35:12.080 --> 00:35:16.850
because if you are in the same
network with the Cloud NAT,

00:35:16.850 --> 00:35:19.190
by default, all traffic
goes through that Cloud NAT.

00:35:19.190 --> 00:35:22.070
And if you have two cloud
NATs in two different regions,

00:35:22.070 --> 00:35:25.380
they start egressing across
regional lines, which is bad.

00:35:25.380 --> 00:35:28.460
So you have to start setting
network tags on your nodes

00:35:28.460 --> 00:35:30.630
to make sure they hit
the Egress traffic.

00:35:30.630 --> 00:35:33.290
So this is just a few of
the little complications

00:35:33.290 --> 00:35:35.720
that we ran into,
and hopefully, we

00:35:35.720 --> 00:35:39.320
can avoid you from running into
them six months down the line.

00:35:39.320 --> 00:35:41.270
So I talked about
private Ingress.

00:35:41.270 --> 00:35:44.660
We have two of the three of the
pieces of the solution built

00:35:44.660 --> 00:35:47.770
out, and have for a
while, and it works OK,

00:35:47.770 --> 00:35:49.670
but we're not in love with it.

00:35:49.670 --> 00:35:53.570
The GCE cloud provider, here,
this is built into Kubernetes,

00:35:53.570 --> 00:35:56.270
and it knows about
GCP, and it configures

00:35:56.270 --> 00:35:59.960
the IRB-- the internal
load balancer in GCP,

00:35:59.960 --> 00:36:06.040
and that allows you to do, I
believe, TCP load balancing.

00:36:06.040 --> 00:36:08.990
It's a layer four
TCP load balancing.

00:36:08.990 --> 00:36:12.080
However, what we needed
was an analogy or something

00:36:12.080 --> 00:36:14.210
analogous to our public
load balancer, which

00:36:14.210 --> 00:36:16.370
does TLS termination.

00:36:16.370 --> 00:36:18.620
So what we ended up doing
was putting Nginx controller

00:36:18.620 --> 00:36:20.070
behind that.

00:36:20.070 --> 00:36:23.240
So we have ILB routing that
Nginx, Nginx does termination,

00:36:23.240 --> 00:36:25.400
and then we handle the
traffic internally,

00:36:25.400 --> 00:36:27.380
and then we're
working on deploying

00:36:27.380 --> 00:36:32.090
Istio to do the end-to-end
internal encryption.

00:36:32.090 --> 00:36:33.810
But the last mile still exists.

00:36:33.810 --> 00:36:35.890
You still have to handle
DNS, like I mentioned.

00:36:35.890 --> 00:36:38.360
There's an external DNS project
that we have on our backlog

00:36:38.360 --> 00:36:39.890
to look into.

00:36:39.890 --> 00:36:44.360
It can configure the private
DNS or public DNS in GCP

00:36:44.360 --> 00:36:46.587
for configuring
those, but it becomes

00:36:46.587 --> 00:36:48.920
a little more complicated if
you have a private network.

00:36:48.920 --> 00:36:52.550
For example, we've been using
route 53 for private DNS

00:36:52.550 --> 00:36:55.380
resolution, and that
works great on AWS,

00:36:55.380 --> 00:36:56.870
but in order to
get that into GCP,

00:36:56.870 --> 00:36:58.578
we have to route
through the interconnect

00:36:58.578 --> 00:37:01.640
to resolve all your DNS
connections, and that's a mess.

00:37:01.640 --> 00:37:04.670
So we ended up caching
that on the GCP side,

00:37:04.670 --> 00:37:08.510
and we're looking into a
more DNS mesh solution, where

00:37:08.510 --> 00:37:13.100
we have some Google Cloud
parts and some AWS parts,

00:37:13.100 --> 00:37:16.460
and they mesh together so that
they have multiple sources

00:37:16.460 --> 00:37:19.450
of truth for the DNS.

00:37:19.450 --> 00:37:21.930
So external DNS won't
solve all of that together,

00:37:21.930 --> 00:37:26.120
but it will at least help you
provision your private DNS.

00:37:26.120 --> 00:37:29.030
So the benefit of
this solution is

00:37:29.030 --> 00:37:30.920
that all three of
those components

00:37:30.920 --> 00:37:35.570
can be set together with
Kubernetes configurations.

00:37:35.570 --> 00:37:37.470
So this is self-service
to the user.

00:37:37.470 --> 00:37:39.740
The user can say I
want private DNS.

00:37:39.740 --> 00:37:42.680
Sorry, I want private Ingress,
or I want public Ingress,

00:37:42.680 --> 00:37:45.810
and I configure that the same
time I deploy my application.

00:37:45.810 --> 00:37:50.330
Which means that I, as a PaaS
owner or a cluster operator,

00:37:50.330 --> 00:37:53.000
don't need to care about how
people are doing Ingress.

00:37:53.000 --> 00:37:56.510
I provide some solutions,
and they opt into them.

00:37:56.510 --> 00:37:58.247
All these logs are
available to them,

00:37:58.247 --> 00:38:00.080
and they can opt into
them with annotations.

00:38:00.080 --> 00:38:02.200
It's pretty slick.

00:38:02.200 --> 00:38:05.020
So we do have some operational
challenges as well.

00:38:05.020 --> 00:38:06.550
When you are in
multiple clusters,

00:38:06.550 --> 00:38:09.310
whether that's multiple
environments or multi-tenancy,

00:38:09.310 --> 00:38:11.560
we have to figure
out whether you

00:38:11.560 --> 00:38:14.750
want to aggregate or separate
your metrics and your logs.

00:38:14.750 --> 00:38:18.700
So by default, GKE logs and
metrics go to the Stackdriver

00:38:18.700 --> 00:38:21.220
in the same GCP project.

00:38:21.220 --> 00:38:24.550
We didn't necessarily
like that very much.

00:38:24.550 --> 00:38:27.100
So what that meant
is we had to give you

00:38:27.100 --> 00:38:31.340
permission to all of the
users on our shared clusters.

00:38:31.340 --> 00:38:33.610
And we didn't want to
give them rate permissions

00:38:33.610 --> 00:38:36.630
because they were
our paths projects,

00:38:36.630 --> 00:38:38.630
and they would step
on each other's toes.

00:38:38.630 --> 00:38:41.440
So what that meant is
we had to sort of invent

00:38:41.440 --> 00:38:44.080
a solution that
would emit metric

00:38:44.080 --> 00:38:47.090
or, sorry, logs
to other projects.

00:38:47.090 --> 00:38:50.050
So we have a Stackdriver
fluent solution

00:38:50.050 --> 00:38:53.170
that will emit a duplicate
of the logs to the projects

00:38:53.170 --> 00:38:55.760
that they own so that they can
do log to metric conversion,

00:38:55.760 --> 00:38:57.640
and they can also
do log dashboards

00:38:57.640 --> 00:39:00.940
or log metric dashboards.

00:39:00.940 --> 00:39:03.430
For logging, we mostly
just use Stackdriver.

00:39:03.430 --> 00:39:07.150
For tracing, we use
Datadog, like I mentioned.

00:39:07.150 --> 00:39:10.330
For uptime checks,
we use Runscope.

00:39:10.330 --> 00:39:12.520
So one of the
benefits of Runscope

00:39:12.520 --> 00:39:16.570
is that unlike most
things like Pingdom

00:39:16.570 --> 00:39:18.520
or other external
SaaS providers that

00:39:18.520 --> 00:39:20.410
will do checks of
your applications,

00:39:20.410 --> 00:39:23.142
Runscope allows you to deploy
an agent behind your firewall.

00:39:23.142 --> 00:39:24.850
So we can do this in
the private network,

00:39:24.850 --> 00:39:28.520
and it does a check
of your application.

00:39:28.520 --> 00:39:32.860
So I'll talk a little bit more
about Runscope in a minute,

00:39:32.860 --> 00:39:35.200
but about availability,
we also provide

00:39:35.200 --> 00:39:36.610
a couple of features
of the paths

00:39:36.610 --> 00:39:38.590
that don't come
native which GKE.

00:39:38.590 --> 00:39:42.080
That includes backups
with Arc, which I believe,

00:39:42.080 --> 00:39:43.570
has a new name that I forget.

00:39:43.570 --> 00:39:45.820
We also do play a descheduler,
which makes things

00:39:45.820 --> 00:39:47.770
evenly deploy across the cloud.

00:39:47.770 --> 00:39:50.620
And like was
mentioned, node pool

00:39:50.620 --> 00:39:53.580
can be upgraded with the
click of a button on GKE.

00:39:53.580 --> 00:39:56.290
Unfortunately,
that only upgrades

00:39:56.290 --> 00:39:58.340
the OS and the
Kubernetes version.

00:39:58.340 --> 00:40:00.310
So if you want to change
the instance type,

00:40:00.310 --> 00:40:02.680
or you want to
change node tags, you

00:40:02.680 --> 00:40:05.540
have to do your own
recreation of the node pool.

00:40:05.540 --> 00:40:07.510
So what we ended up
doing was terraforming

00:40:07.510 --> 00:40:10.210
in a blue/green deployment
of node pools, which

00:40:10.210 --> 00:40:13.210
is a little complicated, but
allows us to do zero downtime

00:40:13.210 --> 00:40:14.050
deployments.

00:40:14.050 --> 00:40:16.430
We have to have double
the quota we normally run,

00:40:16.430 --> 00:40:20.760
which is kind of a
hazard, but it works.

00:40:20.760 --> 00:40:22.280
So a little bit about Runscope.

00:40:22.280 --> 00:40:26.960
We deploy the agents
on GCE and also on GKE.

00:40:26.960 --> 00:40:30.320
We also wrote an operator
for this, which again, will

00:40:30.320 --> 00:40:32.920
probably be open source soon.

00:40:32.920 --> 00:40:37.160
And this operator takes,
currently, Ingress annotations.

00:40:37.160 --> 00:40:39.260
So these Ingress
annotations tell

00:40:39.260 --> 00:40:42.530
what endpoint do I want to
hit, what back end bucket do

00:40:42.530 --> 00:40:44.360
I want to put them
in in Runscope,

00:40:44.360 --> 00:40:47.600
and then Runscope
will tell the agent,

00:40:47.600 --> 00:40:50.457
or rather the agent
asks the SaaS product

00:40:50.457 --> 00:40:51.290
what should I check?

00:40:51.290 --> 00:40:53.123
And then the agent
checks your applications.

00:40:53.123 --> 00:40:55.550
Sometimes that's going
horizontally on the cluster.

00:40:55.550 --> 00:40:57.300
Sometimes that's going
out of the network,

00:40:57.300 --> 00:40:59.550
and back in the Ingress path.

00:40:59.550 --> 00:41:01.550
Sometimes it's going
between clusters, depending

00:41:01.550 --> 00:41:03.120
what you're trying to check.

00:41:03.120 --> 00:41:07.610
So this allows you to check your
applications from the full end

00:41:07.610 --> 00:41:11.450
to end path, including Ingress,
and not just what Kubernetes

00:41:11.450 --> 00:41:16.005
does, which is the cubelet on
the node checking your pod.

00:41:16.005 --> 00:41:17.630
As you can imagine,
that doesn't really

00:41:17.630 --> 00:41:19.520
cover much breadth
of networking,

00:41:19.520 --> 00:41:21.330
and usually networking
is the problem.

00:41:21.330 --> 00:41:24.380
So having something that
will do a sort of end

00:41:24.380 --> 00:41:27.470
to end smoke test for you every
minute or every five minutes

00:41:27.470 --> 00:41:29.780
is super valuable.

00:41:29.780 --> 00:41:33.110
So we plugged this into our
local private networking,

00:41:33.110 --> 00:41:35.810
and also a public networking,
and we have the optional.

00:41:35.810 --> 00:41:37.880
We're going to be working
on converting this

00:41:37.880 --> 00:41:43.640
to a CRD in the future so that
it's not five annotations,

00:41:43.640 --> 00:41:46.790
but that will allow a little
more complicated configurations

00:41:46.790 --> 00:41:47.585
of Runscope tests.

00:41:51.380 --> 00:41:54.530
So the moral of the story here,
a bunch of these operators

00:41:54.530 --> 00:41:59.030
that I've sort of displayed and
invented or worked with my team

00:41:59.030 --> 00:42:02.660
to build has been about
putting your configuration

00:42:02.660 --> 00:42:05.570
for your operations
of your application

00:42:05.570 --> 00:42:07.740
where your application
devs can use it.

00:42:07.740 --> 00:42:09.830
We have a DevOps
model at Cruise,

00:42:09.830 --> 00:42:12.830
where every team that is
deploying or is building code

00:42:12.830 --> 00:42:16.220
is deploying that code,
at least on the back end.

00:42:16.220 --> 00:42:18.770
And so all of our
teams want to be

00:42:18.770 --> 00:42:21.140
able to configure these
things about operations,

00:42:21.140 --> 00:42:23.520
about logging, about
Ingress, about metrics.

00:42:23.520 --> 00:42:25.640
Whether they know
it or not, they

00:42:25.640 --> 00:42:28.460
need access to configure
these things self-service,

00:42:28.460 --> 00:42:30.020
if possible.

00:42:30.020 --> 00:42:33.290
So it creates less
operational overhead for us,

00:42:33.290 --> 00:42:35.480
and it makes things
easier for them.

00:42:35.480 --> 00:42:37.430
They can just go look
at the documentation,

00:42:37.430 --> 00:42:39.860
and figure out how to do things.

00:42:39.860 --> 00:42:42.850
And it makes it easier to do
your continuous deployment

00:42:42.850 --> 00:42:44.600
because your continuous
deployment doesn't

00:42:44.600 --> 00:42:46.767
need to be coupled with all
of these pieces that can

00:42:46.767 --> 00:42:49.720
be independently configured.

00:42:49.720 --> 00:42:51.593
This is the link to
our Cruise blog, where

00:42:51.593 --> 00:42:54.010
you can see those open source
announcements in the future,

00:42:54.010 --> 00:42:56.320
or see our previous
ones already.

00:42:56.320 --> 00:42:59.200
And thank you for coming.

00:42:59.200 --> 00:43:00.400
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:01.900 --> 00:43:04.650
[MUSIC PLAYING]

